summaries={'p0':'A hot Standard Model brane can populate a KK-graviton tower in the specified scenario. The initial abundance question is separate from whether the population survives as dark matter.','p1':'A supplied 2026 analysis reports a d-wave rate proportional to the fifth power of daughter momentum near threshold. Its derivation and full domain of generality remain outside the reading performed here.','p2':'Repeated decays change the tower mass distribution and give daughters recoil velocities. The linear cosmology calculation assumes a specified decay history and does not establish the cascade microphysics.','p3':'A flat-dimension analysis argues that unavoidable production and visible decays constrain reheating and radius even when KK gravitons do not constitute all dark matter. Its numerical exclusion remains an attributed result.','p4':'Production and dilution can impose competing constraints on an early matter-dominated history. Superheavy neutralinos provide a two-stage example; different relics retain their own channels and expansion histories.','p5':'Portal interactions can transfer energy into light hidden species. Estimates that are insensitive to hidden-sector details still require explicit mediator and thermal-history assumptions.','p7':'Historical Auger and HiRes spectra provide separate measurements of high-energy suppression. Suppression consistent with GZK expectations does not uniquely identify its cause or establish a gravitational species scale.','q0':'An electroweak Higgs-portal contribution changes the ALP mass without moving the potential minimum in the supplied setup. The relative timing of oscillation onset and electroweak symmetry breaking must remain explicit.'}
